<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Basutoland</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – 1966 CE · capital Maseru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British territory that preserved Moshoeshoe's mountain kingdom from absorption into South Africa, and became independent Lesotho.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>'s request, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a> in March 1868 to save it from conquest by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a>, which had already taken much of its lowland farmland. The boundaries were fixed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-treaty-of-aliwal-north">Convention of Aliwal North</a> in 1869, and in 1871 the territory was handed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cape attempts to disarm the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> caused the Gun War of 1880-1881, which the Basotho effectively won, and in 1884 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> resumed direct rule as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-commission-territories">High Commission</a> territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maseru">Maseru</a>. Paramount chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>'s line, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letsie-i">Letsie I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lerotholi">Lerotholi</a>, kept wide authority, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basutoland-national-council">Basutoland National Council</a> was created in 1903. Hut tax and poor land made the territory a labour reserve for South African mines.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Basutoland was never incorporated into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>. After the rise of parties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-congress-party">Basutoland Congress Party</a> and constitutional reforms, it became independent as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lesotho">Kingdom of Lesotho</a> on 4 October 1966 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/basutoland.jpg" alt="Basutoland" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1868 CE – 1966 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maseru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Letsie I, Lerotholi, Moshoeshoe II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Sotho traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sesotho, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lesotho">Lesotho</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Basutoland Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1917&amp;focus=basutoland" aria-label="Basutoland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5396 3249 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Basutoland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5716 3505L4997 651M5716 3505L5721 3507M5716 3505L5470 3663M5716 3505L5716 3505"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5721 3507h0M5470 3663h0M5716 3505h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5716 3505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5716 3505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Maseru. <a href="/atlas?year=1917&amp;focus=basutoland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
